Module 6 → Topic 2 → Optional (Part 1)

1. Optional — Introduction

The Billion-Dollar Mistake

Tony Hoare (inventor of null, 1965):

"I call it my billion-dollar mistake. It was the invention of the null reference in 1965."

Null references cause:

NullPointerException — most common Java exception.
Defensive null checks everywhere.
Code clutter.
Silent bugs.

Example — pre-Optional code:

public User getUser(Long id) {
    // Returns null if not found — but documentation might not say this!
    return userRepository.findById(id); // Could return null
}

// Caller:
User user = getUser(1L);
String name = user.getName(); // NullPointerException if getUser returns null!

// Safe caller:
User user = getUser(1L);
if (user != null) {
    String name = user.getName();
    if (name != null) {
        // Use name
    }
}

Null-check pyramid of doom.

Java 8 Solution — Optional

public Optional<User> getUser(Long id) {
    // Return type explicitly says "this might not be present"
    return userRepository.findById(id);
}

// Caller:
Optional<User> optUser = getUser(1L);
optUser.ifPresent(user -> System.out.println(user.getName()));

No NullPointerException possible — Optional forces you to handle absence.

Definition

Optional<T> is a container class in java.util that may or may not contain a non-null value. It is designed to eliminate NullPointerException at API boundaries by making the possible absence of a value explicit in the type system.

2. Creating Optional

Three factory methods:

Optional.of(value)

Use when: Value MUST be non-null.

Optional<String> opt = Optional.of("Hello");
Optional<String> opt = Optional.of(null); // NullPointerException! Fails fast

Optional.ofNullable(value)

Use when: Value MIGHT be null.

Optional<String> opt = Optional.ofNullable("Hello");   // Present
Optional<String> opt = Optional.ofNullable(null);       // Empty

Optional.empty()

Use when: Explicitly returning "no value."

Optional<User> opt = Optional.empty(); // Empty optional

3. Checking and Getting Values

isPresent() / isEmpty()

Optional<String> opt = Optional.of("Hello");

opt.isPresent(); // true
opt.isEmpty();   // false (Java 11+)

get() — Unsafe (avoid)

String value = opt.get(); // Returns value OR throws NoSuchElementException if empty!

// NEVER do this without isPresent() check!
Optional<String> empty = Optional.empty();
empty.get(); // NoSuchElementException — worse than NullPointerException!

orElse(defaultValue) — Safe, always evaluates default

String value = opt.orElse("Default"); // Returns value or "Default"

Optional.empty().orElse("Default"); // "Default"

orElseGet(Supplier) — Safe, lazily evaluates default

// Supplier called ONLY if Optional is empty
String value = opt.orElseGet(() -> computeExpensiveDefault());

// Better than orElse when default is expensive to compute!
User user = optUser.orElseGet(() -> userRepository.findDefaultUser());
// If present: returns user, userRepository NOT called
// If absent: calls userRepository.findDefaultUser()

orElseThrow()

// Java 10+ overload: NoSuchElementException by default
String value = opt.orElseThrow();

// Custom exception (Java 8+):
User user = optUser.orElseThrow(() -> new UserNotFoundException(id));

ifPresent(Consumer) — Execute if value present

optUser.ifPresent(user -> emailService.sendWelcome(user));

ifPresentOrElse(Consumer, Runnable) — Java 9+

optUser.ifPresentOrElse(
    user -> processUser(user),
    () -> log.warn("User not found")
);

4. Transforming Optional — Functional Operations

map(Function) — Transform value if present

Optional<String> name = optUser.map(User::getName);
// If user present: Optional.of(user.getName())
// If user absent: Optional.empty()

// Chaining:
Optional<Integer> nameLength = optUser
    .map(User::getName)
    .map(String::length);

flatMap(Function returning Optional) — Avoid nested Optional

// User has optional email
Optional<Optional<Email>> nested = optUser.map(user -> user.getOptionalEmail()); // BAD!

Optional<Email> flat = optUser.flatMap(user -> user.getOptionalEmail()); // GOOD!

flatMap flattens Optional<Optional<T>> to Optional<T>.

filter(Predicate) — Filter by condition

Optional<User> premiumUser = optUser
    .filter(user -> user.isPremium());
// If user is not premium → Optional.empty()
// If user is premium → same Optional

or(Supplier<Optional<T>>) — Java 9+

Try alternate source if empty:

Optional<User> user = primaryRepository.findByEmail(email)
    .or(() -> secondaryRepository.findByEmail(email))
    .or(() -> Optional.of(defaultUser));

stream() — Java 9+

Convert Optional to Stream:

Stream<User> userStream = optUser.stream();
// Present: Stream with one element
// Empty: empty Stream

// Useful with flatMap:
List<User> users = ids.stream()
    .map(userService::findById)   // Stream<Optional<User>>
    .flatMap(Optional::stream)    // Stream<User> — filters out empties
    .collect(Collectors.toList());

5. Optional Best Practices

Practice 1 — Use as return type, NOT as parameter or field

// GOOD — return type
public Optional<User> findUserById(Long id) { }

// BAD — parameter
public void processUser(Optional<User> user) { } // Don't do this!
// Callers can pass Optional.empty() — use method overloading instead

// BAD — field
public class Service {
    private Optional<Cache> cache; // Don't! Use @Nullable or null directly
}

Practice 2 — Use orElseGet for expensive defaults, orElse for cheap ones

// CHEAP default — orElse fine
String name = optName.orElse("Unknown");

// EXPENSIVE default — orElseGet (lazy evaluation!)
User user = optUser.orElseGet(() -> createDefaultUser()); // Expensive if DB call

Practice 3 — Prefer functional style over isPresent() + get()

// BAD — imperative style
if (optUser.isPresent()) {
    User user = optUser.get();
    processUser(user);
}

// GOOD — functional style
optUser.ifPresent(this::processUser);

// BAD
if (optUser.isPresent()) {
    return optUser.get().getName();
} else {
    return "Unknown";
}

// GOOD
return optUser.map(User::getName).orElse("Unknown");

Practice 4 — Never use Optional.get() without isPresent()

// NEVER
String name = optUser.get().getName(); // NoSuchElementException risk!

// Always use safe accessors
String name = optUser.map(User::getName).orElse("Unknown");

Practice 5 — Don't use Optional for primitive types

// BAD — boxing overhead
Optional<Integer> optInt = Optional.of(42);

// GOOD — use OptionalInt, OptionalLong, OptionalDouble
OptionalInt optInt = OptionalInt.of(42);
int value = optInt.orElse(0);

6. Optional in Spring Data JPA

Spring Data JPA returns Optional:

public interface UserRepository extends JpaRepository<User, Long> {
    Optional<User> findByEmail(String email);
    Optional<User> findByIdAndActive(Long id, boolean active);
}

Usage:

@Service
public class UserService {
    public User getUserById(Long id) {
        return userRepository.findById(id)
            .orElseThrow(() -> new UserNotFoundException(id));
    }

    public Optional<User> findByEmail(String email) {
        return userRepository.findByEmail(email);
    }

    public String getUserDisplayName(Long id) {
        return userRepository.findById(id)
            .map(User::getDisplayName)
            .orElse("Anonymous User");
    }
}

7. Real-World Enterprise Examples

Example 1 — E-Commerce: Product Lookup

@Service
public class ProductService {

    public ProductDetails getProductDetails(Long productId) {
        return productRepository.findById(productId)
            .filter(Product::isActive)
            .map(product -> {
                List<Review> reviews = reviewRepository.findByProduct(product);
                Optional<Price> discount = discountService.findActiveDiscount(productId);
                return new ProductDetails(
                    product,
                    reviews,
                    discount.map(Price::getAmount).orElse(BigDecimal.ZERO)
                );
            })
            .orElseThrow(() -> new ProductNotFoundException(productId));
    }
}

Example 2 — Banking: Configuration Lookup

public class FeatureConfig {

    public boolean isFeatureEnabled(String featureName) {
        return configRepository.findByName(featureName)
            .filter(Config::isActive)
            .map(Config::isEnabled)
            .orElse(false); // Default: disabled if not configured
    }

    public int getTransactionLimit(String accountType) {
        return configRepository.findTransactionLimit(accountType)
            .map(Config::getIntValue)
            .orElseGet(() -> defaultConfigService.getDefaultLimit(accountType));
    }
}

Example 3 — FAANG: User Preferences Cascade

// Try user's custom setting → group default → system default
public Theme getUserTheme(Long userId) {
    return userPreferencesRepo.findByUserId(userId)
        .flatMap(prefs -> prefs.getTheme())           // User's own theme
        .or(() -> groupService.findGroupTheme(userId)) // Group's default
        .orElse(Theme.LIGHT);                          // System default
}

8. ₹5 Crore Interview Answer

Interviewer:

"What is Optional in Java 8 and when should you use it? What are common anti-patterns?"

5 Crore Answer:

Optional<T> is a container introduced in Java 8 that explicitly represents the possible absence of a value. It moves the concept of "this might not exist" from documentation and convention into the type system, making null safety visible and enforceable.

The primary use case is method return types at API boundaries where a value may legitimately be absent — for example, repository methods like findById(). Returning Optional<User> rather than User forces callers to handle the absent case at compile time rather than discovering NullPointerException at runtime.

The key methods are: isPresent()/isEmpty() for checking presence; orElse(default) for safe extraction with a cheap default; orElseGet(Supplier) for expensive defaults evaluated lazily — critically important because orElse() always evaluates the default expression even if the Optional is present; orElseThrow() for failing with a specific exception when absence is unexpected; map(), flatMap(), and filter() for functional transformation pipelines without intermediate null checks; and ifPresent()/ifPresentOrElse() for side effects.

The anti-patterns to avoid: Never use Optional as a method parameter — it complicates APIs since callers might pass Optional.empty() when null should just be rejected. Don't use it as a field type — it's not Serializable and adds unnecessary overhead. Never call get() without checking isPresent() first — this just replaces NullPointerException with NoSuchElementException. Don't wrap primitives in Optional<Integer> — use OptionalInt, OptionalLong, OptionalDouble to avoid boxing overhead.

The most important design insight: Optional is for return types to communicate that absence is a valid, expected outcome — not for general null avoidance. If a null would indicate a programming error (required parameter), throw an exception rather than returning empty. If a null would indicate normal absence (user not found), return Optional.

Spring Data JPA uses Optional throughout — JpaRepository.findById() returns Optional<T>, enabling clean chaining: findById(id).orElseThrow(NotFoundException::new).

9. Follow-Up Interview Questions

Q1. What is the difference between orElse() and orElseGet()?

orElse(T): The default value expression is ALWAYS evaluated.
orElse(expensiveMethod()) — expensiveMethod() called even if Optional is present!

orElseGet(Supplier<T>): Default computed ONLY if Optional is empty. Lazy evaluation.
orElseGet(() -> expensiveMethod()) — expensiveMethod() only called if absent.

Use orElseGet when default computation is expensive (DB call, file read).

Q2. What is the difference between map() and flatMap() on Optional?

map(fn): fn returns a plain value → wraps it in Optional.
Optional<String> → map(fn returning String) → Optional<String>

flatMap(fn): fn returns Optional → doesn't double-wrap.
Optional<User> → flatMap(user → user.getOptionalEmail()) → Optional<Email>
Without flatMap: Optional<Optional<Email>> — nested, unwieldy.

Q3. Should Optional be used as a method parameter?

No. Anti-pattern.

Reasons:
1. Callers should provide the value or null — not wrap in Optional.
2. Optional can hide programming errors (passing Optional.empty() when null should fail validation).
3. Breaks API usability.

If parameter might be optional, use method overloading:

public void process(User user) { }
public void process(User user, Config config) { }  // config is optional here

Q4. Why is Optional not Serializable?

By design decision. Optional is intended only for return types in method signatures.

Making it Serializable would encourage using it as fields in entities or DTOs — an anti-pattern.

If you need an optional value in a serializable class, use @Nullable with null, or use a different pattern.

Q5. Is Optional thread-safe?

Optional is immutable — once created, its value doesn't change.

So yes, Optional instances are thread-safe.

But the values they contain may not be if they're mutable objects.

10. Complete Revision Notes

Optional: container for value that may or may not be present. Eliminates NullPointerException.
Creation: Optional.of(value) (non-null), Optional.ofNullable(value) (nullable), Optional.empty().
get(): returns value or NoSuchElementException. NEVER use without isPresent().
orElse(default): returns value or default. Default ALWAYS evaluated.
orElseGet(Supplier): returns value or Supplier result. Supplier LAZILY evaluated.
orElseThrow(): returns value or throws NoSuchElementException (or custom with Supplier).
isPresent(): true if value present. isEmpty(): true if absent (Java 11+).
ifPresent(Consumer): execute action if present.
ifPresentOrElse(Consumer, Runnable): execute one or other (Java 9+).
map(Function): transform value → Optional of result.
flatMap(Function→Optional): transform, avoid nested Optional.
filter(Predicate): keep or empty based on predicate.
or(Supplier→Optional): provide alternate Optional if empty (Java 9+).
stream(): convert to Stream (Java 9+). Empty Optional → empty Stream.
Anti-pattern: Optional as parameter (reject — use overloading or null check).
Anti-pattern: Optional as field (not Serializable, unnecessary overhead).
Anti-pattern: Optional.get() without isPresent() check.
Anti-pattern: Optional<Integer> — use OptionalInt, OptionalLong, OptionalDouble.
Spring Data: JpaRepository.findById() returns Optional<T>.
Best use: return type where absence is valid expected outcome.
Prefer functional style (map/filter/orElse) over imperative (isPresent + get).

Module 6 → Topic 4 → Pattern Matching (Part 1)

1. Pattern Matching — Introduction

What is Pattern Matching?

Pattern matching = Testing a value against a pattern AND binding the value to a variable if it matches.

Pattern = a shape/type/condition to test.

Before Pattern Matching in Java:

// Old Java — verbose, repetitive, error-prone
if (shape instanceof Circle) {
    Circle circle = (Circle) shape; // Redundant cast!
    double area = Math.PI * circle.getRadius() * circle.getRadius();
}

After Pattern Matching:

// Java 16+ — concise, safe
if (shape instanceof Circle circle) { // Test AND bind in one step
    double area = Math.PI * circle.radius() * circle.radius();
    // No cast needed! circle is already Circle type
}

2. Pattern Matching for instanceof (Java 16)

Syntax:

expr instanceof Type variableName

"expr is an instance of Type AND bind it to variableName"

Example:

Object obj = getObject();

// Old way
if (obj instanceof String) {
    String s = (String) obj; // Redundant cast
    System.out.println(s.length());
}

// New way (Java 16+)
if (obj instanceof String s) {
    System.out.println(s.length()); // s is already String — no cast!
}

Scope Rules:

The bound variable is in scope only where it's known to be true.

if (obj instanceof String s) {
    // s is in scope HERE — obj is String
    System.out.println(s.length());
}
// s is NOT in scope here

Pattern with negation:

if (!(obj instanceof String s)) {
    // s is NOT in scope here (condition might be false)
    throw new IllegalArgumentException("Expected String");
}
// s IS in scope here (negation means we know obj is String)
System.out.println(s.length());

Combining with &&:

if (obj instanceof String s && s.length() > 5) {
    // Both: obj is String AND length > 5
    System.out.println("Long string: " + s);
}

3. Switch Expressions (Java 14) — Foundation

Switch expressions return a value. Much cleaner than switch statements.

// Old switch statement — verbose, fall-through, no value
int result;
switch (day) {
    case MONDAY:
    case TUESDAY:
    case WEDNESDAY:
    case THURSDAY:
    case FRIDAY:
        result = 8;
        break;
    case SATURDAY:
    case SUNDAY:
        result = 0;
        break;
    default:
        throw new IllegalStateException();
}

// Switch expression — concise, no fall-through, returns value
int result = switch (day) {
    case MONDAY, TUESDAY, WEDNESDAY, THURSDAY, FRIDAY -> 8;
    case SATURDAY, SUNDAY -> 0;
};

Arrow form: case X -> expression (no fall-through, returns value).

Colon form (for complex blocks):

int result = switch (day) {
    case MONDAY, TUESDAY -> {
        log.info("Weekday: " + day);
        yield 8; // yield returns value from block
    }
    case SATURDAY, SUNDAY -> 0;
};

yield: Returns value from a switch block. (Like break but with value.)

4. Pattern Matching for Switch (Java 21)

The biggest language feature.

Combines: switch expression + type patterns + guarded patterns + sealed class exhaustiveness.

Syntax:

result = switch (shape) {
    case Circle c -> Math.PI * c.radius() * c.radius();
    case Rectangle r -> r.width() * r.height();
    case null -> 0; // explicit null handling
    default -> throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unknown: " + shape);
};

Type patterns in switch:

Object obj = getValue();

String description = switch (obj) {
    case Integer i -> "Integer: " + i;
    case String s -> "String: " + s;
    case List<?> l -> "List of " + l.size() + " elements";
    case null -> "null value";
    default -> "Unknown type: " + obj.getClass().getSimpleName();
};

Guarded Patterns — when clause:

String classify(Object obj) {
    return switch (obj) {
        case Integer i when i < 0  -> "negative integer";
        case Integer i when i == 0 -> "zero";
        case Integer i             -> "positive integer";  // fallthrough from above guards
        case String s when s.isEmpty() -> "empty string";
        case String s              -> "string: " + s;
        default -> "other";
    };
}

Sealed classes — exhaustive switch:

sealed interface Shape permits Circle, Rectangle, Triangle {}

// Compiler knows all cases — no default needed
double area(Shape shape) {
    return switch (shape) {
        case Circle c    -> Math.PI * c.radius() * c.radius();
        case Rectangle r -> r.width() * r.height();
        case Triangle t  -> 0.5 * t.base() * t.height();
        // No default — compiler verifies exhaustiveness
    };
}

5. Deconstruction Patterns (Java 21)

Record patterns — destructure record components directly in pattern:

record Point(int x, int y) {}
record Line(Point start, Point end) {}

Object obj = new Line(new Point(1, 2), new Point(3, 4));

// Old way
if (obj instanceof Line line) {
    Point start = line.start();
    Point end = line.end();
    System.out.println("Start: " + start.x() + "," + start.y());
}

// Record pattern — destructure directly
if (obj instanceof Line(Point start, Point end)) {
    System.out.println("Start: " + start.x() + "," + start.y());
}

// Nested patterns — destructure nested records
if (obj instanceof Line(Point(int x1, int y1), Point(int x2, int y2))) {
    System.out.println("From (" + x1 + "," + y1 + ") to (" + x2 + "," + y2 + ")");
}

// In switch:
double length(Object obj) {
    return switch (obj) {
        case Line(Point(int x1, int y1), Point(int x2, int y2)) ->
            Math.sqrt(Math.pow(x2-x1, 2) + Math.pow(y2-y1, 2));
        case Point(int x, int y) ->
            Math.sqrt(x*x + y*y);
        default -> throw new IllegalArgumentException();
    };
}

6. Real-World Enterprise Examples

Example 1 — Payment Processing (Sealed + Pattern Matching)

sealed interface PaymentEvent
    permits PaymentEvent.Initiated, PaymentEvent.Completed, PaymentEvent.Failed, PaymentEvent.Refunded {

    record Initiated(String orderId, BigDecimal amount, String currency) implements PaymentEvent {}
    record Completed(String orderId, String transactionId, Instant completedAt) implements PaymentEvent {}
    record Failed(String orderId, String errorCode, String message) implements PaymentEvent {}
    record Refunded(String orderId, String transactionId, BigDecimal refundAmount) implements PaymentEvent {}
}

// Handler — exhaustive, type-safe
void handlePaymentEvent(PaymentEvent event) {
    switch (event) {
        case PaymentEvent.Initiated(var orderId, var amount, var currency) -> {
            log.info("Payment initiated for order {} — {} {}", orderId, amount, currency);
            metricsService.recordInitiation(amount);
        }
        case PaymentEvent.Completed(var orderId, var txId, var completedAt) -> {
            orderService.markPaid(orderId);
            notificationService.sendReceipt(orderId, txId);
            log.info("Payment completed for {} at {}", orderId, completedAt);
        }
        case PaymentEvent.Failed(var orderId, var code, var msg) -> {
            orderService.markPaymentFailed(orderId);
            alertingService.sendPaymentFailureAlert(orderId, code, msg);
        }
        case PaymentEvent.Refunded(var orderId, var txId, var amount) -> {
            orderService.recordRefund(orderId, amount);
            notificationService.sendRefundConfirmation(orderId, txId, amount);
        }
    }
}

Example 2 — Type-Safe JSON Processing

Object parseJson(JsonNode node) {
    return switch (node) {
        case TextNode t -> t.textValue();
        case IntNode i -> i.intValue();
        case DoubleNode d -> d.doubleValue();
        case ArrayNode a -> a.elements().toList().stream()
            .map(this::parseJson).toList();
        case ObjectNode o -> {
            Map<String, Object> map = new LinkedHashMap<>();
            o.fields().forEachRemaining(e -> map.put(e.getKey(), parseJson(e.getValue())));
            yield map;
        }
        case NullNode n -> null;
        default -> throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unsupported: " + node.getClass());
    };
}

Example 3 — Expression Tree Evaluation

sealed interface Expr
    permits Num, Add, Multiply, Negate {
    record Num(double value) implements Expr {}
    record Add(Expr left, Expr right) implements Expr {}
    record Multiply(Expr left, Expr right) implements Expr {}
    record Negate(Expr expr) implements Expr {}
}

// Recursive evaluation using pattern matching
double eval(Expr expr) {
    return switch (expr) {
        case Num(double v) -> v;
        case Add(Expr l, Expr r) -> eval(l) + eval(r);
        case Multiply(Expr l, Expr r) -> eval(l) * eval(r);
        case Negate(Expr e) -> -eval(e);
    };
}

// Usage: (3 + 4) * -(2)
Expr calculation = new Multiply(
    new Add(new Num(3), new Num(4)),
    new Negate(new Num(2))
);
double result = eval(calculation); // -14.0

7. ₹5 Crore Interview Answer

Interviewer:

"Explain Java's evolution of pattern matching from Java 14 to 21. What makes it powerful?"

5 Crore Answer:

Java's pattern matching evolution spans several releases, each building on the previous.

Java 14 introduced switch expressions — switch constructs that return values, with arrow syntax eliminating fall-through and making case groups cleaner. This is the syntactic foundation.

Java 16 brought pattern matching for instanceof — instead of the traditional instanceof check followed by an explicit cast, you can now write instanceof Type binding in one expression. The compiler tracks that within the true branch, the binding variable is definitively typed — no cast, no unsafe operation.

Java 21 is where pattern matching becomes truly powerful. Switch expressions gained type pattern support — each case can be a type pattern, matching and binding simultaneously. Guarded patterns add when clauses to add conditions alongside type patterns. Sealed classes with pattern matching switch achieve exhaustiveness: the compiler knows all permitted subtypes and requires all to be handled, generating a compile error if any are missing.

Java 21 also introduced record deconstruction patterns — within a pattern, record components can be recursively destructured. case Line(Point start, Point end) in a switch will both match a Line and destructure its start and end fields, which themselves can be further destructured.

The power comes from combining these features: sealed type hierarchy + record components + exhaustive pattern switch = algebraic data types with pattern matching, bringing to Java what languages like Haskell, Scala, Rust, and Kotlin have had for years.

In enterprise code, this eliminates entire categories of runtime errors. A PaymentResult sealed interface with Completed, Declined, Error subtypes — processed with an exhaustive switch — means it's physically impossible to forget the Declined case. Refactoring — adding a new subtype — automatically surfaces all sites that need updating as compile errors. This is a fundamental improvement in correctness guarantees.

8. Complete Revision Notes

Pattern matching: test value against pattern + bind to typed variable in one step.
instanceof pattern (Java 16): if (obj instanceof String s) — binds s if obj is String.
Scope: bound variable in scope only where the pattern is true.
Negation: if (!(obj instanceof String s)) — s in scope AFTER this check.
Guard: if (obj instanceof String s && s.length() > 5) — both conditions.
Switch expression (Java 14): returns value, arrow syntax, no fall-through, yield for blocks.
yield: returns value from switch block (like break with value).
Switch pattern matching (Java 21): type patterns in case clauses.
Guarded patterns: case Integer i when i > 0 → condition on matched type.
Sealed + switch: exhaustive matching — no default needed, compiler verifies all cases.
Null handling in switch: case null → handle null explicitly.
Record patterns (Java 21): case Line(Point start, Point end) — destructure components.
Nested record patterns: case Line(Point(int x1, int y1), Point(int x2, int y2)).
Enterprise use: PaymentResult sealed hierarchy → exhaustive handling → correctness guarantee.
Expression evaluators: sealed Expr hierarchy → recursive pattern matching evaluator.
Type-safe JSON: dispatch on JsonNode subtypes via pattern matching.
Java version summary: 14 (switch expr), 16 (instanceof pattern), 17 (sealed stable), 21 (switch patterns + record patterns stable).
Algebraic data types: sealed (sum types) + records (product types) + pattern matching.
Guarded patterns replace: instanceof chain with multiple conditions.
All of these features together: Rust-like ergonomics for Java.
